# Rust coding standards

Full guide: [docs/rust-best-practices.md](docs/rust-best-practices.md)
Upstream: https://canonical.github.io/rust-best-practices/introduction.html

## Must follow

- `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace`
- `mod.rs` only declares modules / re-exports (no logic)
- Library: `Error` + `Result` in `lib.rs` right after `mod`/`use`
- Binary: `error.rs` + `result.rs` at crate root (`strimzi-ops`)
- Concrete `thiserror` in libraries — **no** `anyhow` / `Box<dyn Error>` in `strimzi-ops-core`
- Convert foreign errors at the boundary; messages like `cannot …` (lowercase unless acronyms)
- No `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` outside tests or true programmer errors (e.g. static regex)
- Prefer `Self` in inherent impls; tight mutability scopes
- For `Result<()>` paths: `?` then explicit `Ok(())`

## Examples

```rust
// ❌ BAD — panic on user/IO path
let text = fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap();

// ✅ GOOD
let text = fs::read_to_string(path).map_err(|source| Error::Read {
    path: path.to_path_buf(),
    source,
})?;
```

```rust
// ❌ BAD — logic in mod.rs
// ✅ GOOD — mod.rs only:
pub mod config;
pub use config::LinterConfig;
```
